## Company Overview

Airfoils, Incorporated is an aerodynamic consulting firm specializing in
airfoil design, analysis, and wind-tunnel testing. Founded in 1980 by Dan M.
Somers, the firm serves clients in wind energy, aviation, defense, and
industrial applications. The company is based in Port Matilda, Pennsylvania.

The firm's core methodology uses the Eppler Airfoil Design and Analysis Code,
which combines three methods:
1. A conformal-mapping method for designing airfoils with prescribed
   velocity-distribution characteristics
2. A panel method for analyzing the potential flow about given airfoils
3. An integral boundary-layer method for analyzing the development of the
   laminar and turbulent boundary layers and their interaction with the
   inviscid flow

## About Dan M. Somers

Dan M. Somers is an internationally recognized expert in airfoil design,
analysis, and testing with over 40 years of experience.

Education:
- B.S. in Aeronautical Engineering, Purdue University, 1974

Employment:
- President, Airfoils, Incorporated, 1980 to present
- Research Engineer, NASA Langley Research Center, 1974 to 1989

Awards:
- 1991 Soaring Society of America Exceptional Service Award
- 1991 R&D 100 Award for Development of Advanced Wind Turbine Blades
  (with James L. Tangler and James V. Frerotte)
- 1984 NASA Langley Floyd L. Thompson Fellowship for Medium-Speed,
  Natural-Laminar-Flow Airfoil Design

Airfoils designed by Dan Somers include:
- S801 through S829, S901-S903 airfoils for horizontal-axis wind turbines
- S904, S905 airfoils for cooling-tower fans
- S101, S102 airfoils for general aviation aircraft
- S201 airfoil for a cruise missile
- S202 airfoil for a tilt-wing aircraft (including high-lift systems)
- S203 airfoil for a business jet
- S401, S402 airfoils for a gyroplane
- S601, S602 airfoils for unmanned aerial vehicles
- SM701 airfoil for a sailplane
- NASA NLF(1)-0416, NLF(1)-0215F, NLF(1)-0315, NLF(1)-0115 airfoils for
  general aviation
- NASA NLF(1)-1015 airfoil for long-endurance aircraft
- NASA/DLR NLF(2)-0415 airfoil for commuter aircraft

Wind-tunnel investigations conducted at:
- NASA Langley Low-Turbulence Pressure Tunnel (LTPT)
- Delft University of Technology Low Speed Laboratory
- The Pennsylvania State University low-speed, low-turbulence wind tunnel
- DLR Transonic Wind Tunnel Braunschweig (TWB)

